't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-RqMU21JE' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-09-04 20:13:32.929 xcodebuild[93681:13354545]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-04 20:13:33.094 xcodebuild[93681:13354544] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ndiff --git a/docs/agents/team/TEAM.md b/docs/agents/team/TEAM.md\nindex 6dd1b89..8103f8d 100644\n--- a/docs/agents/team/TEAM.md\n+++ b/docs/agents/team/TEAM.md\n@@ -9,9 +9,16 @@ agents. Coordination is through repository artifacts, not hidden context.\n \n ## Roster\n \n+The Orchestrator is deliberately absent from this table. It is not routed and has\n+no primary or fallback: it is whichever coding agent the user starts and assigns\n+the role to. Judge a seat by the four capabilities in\n+[`orchestrator.md`](./orchestrator.md) — read the tree, write files, run each\n+host's suite, shell out to the delegate CLIs — never by roster position. A seat\n+that cannot run the suites cannot verify, whatever model fills it; the sandboxed\n+AGY pitfall in the invocation reference is the worked example.\n+\n | Role                | Primary            | Fallbacks                                                          | Responsibility                                               |\n |---------------------|--------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n-| Orchestrator        | `gpt-5.6-sol`      | `claude-sonnet-5`                                                  | Scope, delegation, consensus, verification, commit readiness |\n | Architect           | `claude-fable-5-1` | `gpt-5.6-sol`, `claude-opus-5`                                     | Axioms, invariants, boundaries, architecture                 |\n | VM Runtime          | `glm-5.3`          | `claude-sonnet-5`, `gpt-5.6-terra`                                 | CESK, VMs, continuations, macros, loops                      |\n | Storage & Indexing  | `glm-5.3`          | `claude-sonnet-5`, `deepseek-v4-pro`                               | B-trees, indexing, DHT, storage, query                       |\n@@ -28,10 +35,7 @@ Any model may fill any role; promote/demote using representative work, findings,\n tests, latency, and cost. `gemini-3.8-flash` supersedes 3.7; `claude-fable-5-1`\n supersedes fable-5. Use the verified `claude-sonnet-5` identifier; the reversed\n `claude-5-sonnet` form is not recognized by Claude Code. The 2026-08-29 GLM\n-quota override expired 2026-09-01. `gemini-3.1-pro-high` was removed from the\n-Orchestrator fallbacks on 2026-09-04: it runs only through sandboxed AGY, which\n-cannot execute this host's JVM, so it cannot verify a test result — see the\n-pitfall in the invocation reference before restoring it.\n+quota override expired 2026-09-01.\n \n ## Routing and independence\n \n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Team roster, routing, collaboration, and invocation rules for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD SOFTWARE ENGINEERING TEAM\n     6\t\n     7\tCanonical guide for assigning, briefing, invoking, reviewing, and recording\n     8\tagents. Coordination is through repository artifacts, not hidden context.\n     9\t\n    10\t## Roster\n    11\t\n    12\tThe Orchestrator is deliberately absent from this table. It is not routed and has\n    13\tno primary or fallback: it is whichever coding agent the user starts and assigns\n    14\tthe role to. Judge a seat by the four capabilities in\n    15\t[`orchestrator.md`](./orchestrator.md) — read the tree, write files, run each\n    16\thost's suite, shell out to the delegate CLIs — never by roster position. A seat\n    17\tthat cannot run the suites cannot verify, whatever model fills it; the sandboxed\n    18\tAGY pitfall in the invocation reference is the worked example.\n    19\t\n    20\t| Role                | Primary            | Fallbacks                                                          | Responsibility                                               |\n    21\t|---------------------|--------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n    22\t| Architect           | `claude-fable-5-1` | `gpt-5.6-sol`, `claude-opus-5`                                     | Axioms, invariants, boundaries, architecture                 |\n    23\t| VM Runtime          | `glm-5.3`          | `claude-sonnet-5`, `gpt-5.6-terra`                                 | CESK, VMs, continuations, macros, loops                      |\n    24\t| Storage & Indexing  | `glm-5.3`          | `claude-sonnet-5`, `deepseek-v4-pro`                               | B-trees, indexing, DHT, storage, query                       |\n    25\t| Compiler & AST      | `claude-opus-5`    | `gpt-5.6-sol`, `glm-5.3`, `gpt-5.6-terra`, `qwen/qwen3.8-max`      | AST, lowering, compile-time macros                           |\n    26\t| Stream & Network    | `claude-opus-5`    | `glm-5.3`, `gpt-5.6-terra`, `gpt-5.6-luna`, `deepseek-v4-pro`      | Streams, framing, concurrency, transports, codecs, RPC       |\n    27\t| Frontend & Graphics | `gpt-5.6-sol`      | `claude-opus-5`, `gemini-3.8-flash`, `moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code`   | Events, WebGL/WebGPU, canvas, terminal                       |\n    28\t| QA & Verification   | `claude-sonnet-5`  | `gemini-3.8-flash`, `gpt-5.4-mini`                                 | TDD, parity, lint, regression                                |\n    29\t| Routine Review      | `gpt-5.6-sol`      | `gemini-3.8-flash`, `glm-5.3` (non-GLM only), `qwen/qwen3.8-max`   | Correctness, invariants, portability                         |\n    30\t| Adversarial Review  | `deepseek-v4-pro`  | `gpt-5.6-sol`, `gemini-3.1-pro-high`                               | Independent defect discovery, cross-host challenge           |\n    31\t| Security Sign-off   | `claude-fable-5-1` | `gpt-5.6-sol`, `claude-opus-5`                                     | Capability boundaries, high-risk review                      |\n    32\t| Scoped / Subagent   | `gemini-3.8-flash` | `gpt-5.6-terra`, `gpt-5.4-mini`                                    | Bounded searches, edits, docs, lint                          |\n    33\t\n    34\tAny model may fill any role; promote/demote using representative work, findings,\n    35\ttests, latency, and cost. `gemini-3.8-flash` supersedes 3.7; `claude-fable-5-1`\n    36\tsupersedes fable-5. Use the verified `claude-sonnet-5` identifier; the reversed\n    37\t`claude-5-sonnet` form is not recognized by Claude Code. The 2026-08-29 GLM\n    38\tquota override expired 2026-09-01.\n    39\t\n    40\t## Routing and independence\n    41\t\n    42\tPrefer flat subscriptions (`claude`, `agy`, `codex`, `glm`); Command Code is\n    43\topportunistic. Reserve Muse/DeepSeek for work worth metered cost. Confirm a model\n    44\texists through its CLI and use a listed fallback—never silently substitute a\n    45\tsame-family reviewer.\n    46\t\n    47\tEvery change is reviewed by a different family: GPT→Gemini/Claude/GLM/Qwen,\n    48\tGLM→GPT/Claude/Gemini/Qwen, Claude→GPT/Gemini/GLM/Qwen, Gemini→GPT/Claude/GLM/Qwen.\n    49\tRoutine review applies only when independent; architectural/security review is\n    50\tmandatory when the role/risk requires it.\n    51\t\n    52\tProvider rules: Claude only via `claude`/`agy`; Muse only via\n    53\t`~/.local/bin/muse` with `muse-spark-1.3-contributor`; never use AGY\n    54\t`invoke_subagent`; shell out to the listed CLIs. The interactive session is the\n    55\tactual orchestrator. GLM peak hours are weekdays 14:00–18:00 UTC+8; schedule\n    56\tlarge jobs off-peak when possible.\n    57\t\n    58\t## Collaboration contract\n    59\t\n    60\tThe orchestrator owns scope, authorization, verification, consensus, and readiness.\n    61\t\n    62\t1. Define behavior with failing tests or an equally precise contract.\n    63\t2. Create a timestamped brief from the role template; bound files and checks.\n    64\t3. Inspect the real diff and verify locally.\n    65\t4. Obtain independent review; reconcile findings and resume the same reviewer for corrections.\n    66\t5. Report risks/readiness. Never stage/commit without user instruction; when authorized,\n    67\t   stage only requested files and commit only staged changes.\n    68\t\n    69\tCommit subjects use `<type>[(<scope>)]: <lowercase imperative summary>` with types\n    70\t`docs|feat|fix|refactor|perf|test|build|chore`; optional body explains non-obvious\n    71\tbehavior/invariants. No trailing period, merge exceptions allowed, never add\n    72\t`Co-Authored-By` (including LLM attribution).\n    73\t\n    74\tDelegated claims are untrusted: verify artifacts, expected files, actual tests and\n    75\tassertion counts, and fresh generated CLJD output; read unfiltered output first.\n    76\tIf local tests already pass, ask reviewers to use budget for static analysis rather\n    77\tthan rerun the full suite (security may rerun). See `build-n-test.md`.\n    78\t\n    79\t## Artifacts and sessions\n    80\t\n    81\tKeep all artifacts flat under `collab/`:\n    82\t`<role>-<task>.prompt.md`, `.<sanitized-model>.findings.md`, `.stdout.log`,\n    83\t`.heartbeat` (optional append-only `.progress.log`). Every prompt starts with:\n    84\t\n    85\t```text\n    86\tCreated-GMT: <actual timestamp>\n    87\tCreated-Local: <actual timestamp and named timezone>\n    88\tCoding-Agent: <claude|codex|agy|glm|cmd|muse|deepseek|interactive>\n    89\tSession-ID: <exact caller UUID | pending (provider-generated)>\n    90\t# Task: <name>\n    91\tRole: <constant role>\n    92\tImplementers:\n    93\t- Model: <model> | Assigned: <timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\n    94\t```\n    95\t\n    96\tEvery report starts with the same Completed-GMT/Local, Coding-Agent, and exact\n    97\tSession-ID fields. For every Claude Code-based CLI (`claude`, `glm`, `deepseek`,\n    98\tand `muse`), generate the UUID before launch, put it in the prompt, and pass it\n    99\twith `--session-id`; text output does not expose the ID reliably. `--name` is a\n   100\tdisplay label, not a session ID. Codex, AGY, and Command Code generate the ID\n   101\tthemselves; their initial prompt records `Session-ID: pending\n   102\t(provider-generated)`, their structured output captures it, and every report\n   103\tand follow-up uses the exact captured value. Because the agent cannot know an\n   104\tID assigned outside its turn, the orchestrator writes the promoted findings\n   105\theader with the captured ID. Never record `none` merely because\n   106\tplain-text output omitted session metadata. `collab/` is append-only, never\n   107\tstaged/committed; never delete or truncate prompts/findings. Artifacts of\n   108\tcommitted work are moved, never deleted, into the flat gitignored `archive/`;\n   109\tthat lifecycle is owned by the role that commits, in\n   110\t[`orchestrator.md`](./orchestrator.md). On reassignment\n   111\tupdate status and append an implementer. Promote final responses to\n   112\t`.findings.md`; `.stdout.log` is only an intermediate capture. Non-trivial\n   113\tdelegates maintain a concise heartbeat. Use actual timestamps; never fabricate\n   114\tthem.\n   115\t\n   116\tRead-only reviewers may share the main tree. Concurrent editors use separate\n   117\tworktrees from a committed base; uncommitted bases require serialization or\n   118\texplicit disjoint ownership. Only one process owns the CLJD lane because\n   119\t`bb test:cljd` writes shared generated output. Auxiliary worktrees lack `collab/`,\n   120\tso briefs use absolute paths. Never merge/delete worktrees or branches without\n   121\tuser authority.\n   122\t\n   123\tQuiet output is not failure: inspect process/heartbeat and wait for completion or\n   124\tan explicit error. Batch complete briefs, reuse sessions for related follow-ups,\n   125\tstart new sessions for unrelated work, and never rely on `--last`.\n   126\t\n   127\t### Session continuity\n   128\t\n   129\tAgents preserve conversational context only when a related follow-up resumes\n   130\tthe exact session/conversation/thread ID. Reading earlier prompts, logs,\n   131\tfindings, and diffs reconstructs task context but is not equivalent to resuming\n   132\tthe session.\n   133\t\n   134\tBefore the first invocation, generate and record an ID:\n   135\t\n   136\t```sh\n   137\tTASK_SESSION_ID=\"$(uuidgen | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')\"\n   138\t```\n   139\t\n   140\tWrite that exact value into the prompt's `Session-ID:` field, then invoke the\n   141\tagent with `--session-id \"$TASK_SESSION_ID\"`. Instruct its report to repeat the\n   142\tsame value. For every correction, clarification, or verification performed by\n   143\tthe same agent, use `--resume \"$TASK_SESSION_ID\"`; do not start a new named\n   144\tsession. Do not combine `--session-id` and `--resume`.\n   145\t\n   146\tClaude Code session persistence is enabled by default. Never pass\n   147\t`--no-session-persistence` when the work may require review, correction, or\n   148\tfollow-up. If an older run failed to record its UUID, recover it from the\n   149\tprovider-specific Claude configuration store by matching the custom title in\n   150\tthe project JSONL, record an append-only provenance correction, and resume that\n   151\tUUID. Do not treat the process ID, `--name`, log filename, or wrapper name as a\n   152\tsession ID.\n   153\t\n   154\tCodex, AGY, and Command Code do not accept a caller-selected ID for a new run in\n   155\tthe installed versions. Run them with structured output, capture the generated\n   156\tID immediately, and put it in the findings before any follow-up:\n   157\t\n   158\t```sh\n   159\t# Codex JSONL: first thread.started event\n   160\tTASK_SESSION_ID=\"$(jq -r 'select(.type == \"thread.started\") | .thread_id' \\\n   161\t  collab/<task>.<model>.stdout.log | head -1)\"\n   162\t\n   163\t# AGY JSON: top-level conversation_id\n   164\tTASK_SESSION_ID=\"$(jq -r '.conversation_id' \\\n   165\t  collab/<task>.<model>.stdout.log)\"\n   166\t\n   167\t# Command Code NDJSON: final result (also present on event.run_start)\n   168\tTASK_SESSION_ID=\"$(jq -r 'select(.type == \"result\") | .sessionId' \\\n   169\t  collab/<task>.<model>.stdout.log | tail -1)\"\n   170\t```\n   171\t\n   172\tFail the handoff if the extracted value is empty or `null`. Do not use Codex\n   173\t`--ephemeral`, Command Code `--no-session`, or any provider's non-persistent\n   174\tmode for follow-up-capable work. Never substitute `--last`, `--continue`, AGY\n   175\t`-c`, or a display name when an exact ID is available: concurrent runs make\n   176\tthose selectors ambiguous.\n   177\t\n   178\tEvery resumed turn gets a new prompt and output artifact name (for example,\n   179\t`<task>-r2...`); never redirect a follow-up into the prior append-only log.\n   180\t\n   181\tReviewer conversations are the ones most worth resuming. A reviewer that has\n   182\talready examined a subsystem retains its seams, invariants, and prior findings,\n   183\tso a re-review costs a delta instead of a cold re-derivation of the same\n   184\tarchitecture. Route a later review of the same subsystem back into its existing\n   185\tconversation whenever that reviewer's family is still independent of the new\n   186\tchange's author; start a fresh conversation only when independence or subject\n   187\tactually changes.\n   188\t\n   189\tAn ID that a run failed to capture is not lost: recover it from the provider's\n   190\tsession store rather than paying for a cold re-review. AGY names each store\n   191\tdirectory after its conversation ID, so the task's own brief locates it:\n   192\t\n   193\t```sh\n   194\tgrep -l \"<task>\" ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/*/.system_generated/logs/transcript.jsonl \\\n   195\t  | sed 's|.*/brain/||; s|/.system_generated.*||'\n   196\t```\n   197\t\n   198\tRecord a recovered value with an append-only provenance correction, as for a\n   199\tClaude UUID, and resume it.\n   200\t\n   201\t| CLI      | Session store                         | New-session ID source                 | Related follow-up                         |\n   202\t|----------|---------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|\n   203\t| claude   | `~/.claude`                           | caller UUID via `--session-id`        | `--resume <uuid>`                         |\n   204\t| glm      | `~/.claude-glm`                       | caller UUID via `--session-id`        | `--resume <uuid>`                         |\n   205\t| deepseek | `~/.claude-deepseek`                  | caller UUID via `--session-id`        | `--resume <uuid>`                         |\n   206\t| muse     | `~/.claude-muse`                      | caller UUID via `--session-id`        | `--resume <uuid>`                         |\n   207\t| codex    | `~/.codex`                            | JSONL `thread.started.thread_id`       | `codex exec resume <id> --json -`          |\n   208\t| agy      | `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli`           | JSON `conversation_id`                | `--conversation <id>`                     |\n   209\t| cmd      | `~/.commandcode`                      | NDJSON `result.sessionId`             | `--resume <id>` or `--session <id|path>`  |\n   210\t\n   211\t## Security\n   212\t\n   213\tPrivate repository content may be sent externally only with explicit user\n   214\tauthorization for the exact payload/destination. Invoke from the authorized\n   215\tagent; consent does not carry over. Pass prompt paths/stdin, never private diffs\n   216\tor credentials in arguments. Use read-only/plan review and minimum write scope;\n   217\tnever bypass permission checks. Preserve unrelated changes and never expose or\n   218\tcommit tokens/configuration.\n   219\t\n   220\t## Invocation reference\n   221\t\n   222\tPrompts contain authorized paths, not source text. Headless plan agents must be\n   223\ttold to produce the complete deliverable without waiting for a human.\n   224\t\n   225\t`claude`, `glm`, `deepseek` and `muse` are one Claude Code CLI behind different\n   226\tmodel wrappers, so none of them needs a PTY and all accept redirected stdin\n   227\tunder `-p`; the `script -q /dev/null` prefix once carried here was unnecessary.\n   228\tVerified 2026-09-04 against `glm`, which ran headless with no PTY and again with\n   229\t`< /dev/null`; the `deepseek` recipe below already closes stdin without a PTY.\n   230\tTheir `claude-code:unrecognized_model` startup warnings follow from that shared\n   231\tbasis.\n   232\t\n   233\t```sh\n   234\t# Claude\n   235\tclaude --model <model> --session-id <uuid> --name <task> \\\n   236\t  --permission-mode plan --tools Read \\\n   237\t  --output-format text -p \"Read <prompt> and complete it now.\"\n   238\t\n   239\t# Claude follow-up: preserve the original model conversation\n   240\tclaude --resume <uuid> --permission-mode plan --tools Read \\\n   241\t  --output-format text -p \"Read <follow-up-prompt> and complete it now.\"\n   242\t\n   243\t# Gemini / AGY\n   244\tagy --model <model> --effort <effort> --mode plan --sandbox \\\n   245\t  --print-timeout 5m --output-format json \\\n   246\t  -p \"Read <prompt> and complete it now.\" > collab/<task>.<model>.stdout.log\n   247\t\n   248\t# AGY follow-up: preserve the generated conversation ID\n   249\tagy --conversation <id> --model <model> --effort <effort> \\\n   250\t  --mode plan --sandbox --print-timeout 5m --output-format json \\\n   251\t  -p \"Read <follow-up-prompt> and complete it now.\" > collab/<task>-r<n>.<model>.stdout.log\n   252\t\n   253\t# GLM (Claude Code-based; keep -p last)\n   254\tGLM_MODEL=glm-5.3 ~/.local/bin/glm \\\n   255\t  --session-id <uuid> --name <task> --bare \\\n   256\t  --permission-mode plan --allowed-tools Read \\\n   257\t  --output-format text -p \"Read <prompt> and complete it now.\" > collab/<task>.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n   258\t\n   259\t# GLM follow-up: keep GLM_MODEL and the original UUID\n   260\tGLM_MODEL=glm-5.3 ~/.local/bin/glm \\\n   261\t  --resume <uuid> --bare --permission-mode plan --allowed-tools Read \\\n   262\t  --output-format text -p \"Read <follow-up-prompt> and complete it now.\" > collab/<task>-r<n>.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n   263\t\n   264\t# Codex review (stdin; read-only; JSONL captures thread_id)\n   265\tcodex exec -m gpt-5.6-sol -s read-only --json - < <prompt> \\\n   266\t  > collab/<task>.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log\n   267\t\n   268\t# Codex follow-up: `resume` exposes no sandbox flag. Resume only a thread that\n   269\t# was created read-only; if its effective policy cannot be verified, start a\n   270\t# new read-only review thread instead of implying an override here.\n   271\tcodex exec resume <id> --json - \\\n   272\t  < <follow-up-prompt> > collab/<task>-r<n>.gpt-5.6-sol.stdout.log\n   273\t\n   274\t# Muse (Claude Code-based; keep -p last)\n   275\tMUSE_MODEL=muse-spark-1.3-contributor ~/.local/bin/muse \\\n   276\t  --session-id <uuid> --name <task> --bare \\\n   277\t  --permission-mode plan --allowed-tools Read \\\n   278\t  --output-format text -p \"Read <prompt> and complete it now.\" > collab/<task>.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n   279\t\n   280\t# Muse follow-up: keep MUSE_MODEL and the original UUID\n   281\tMUSE_MODEL=muse-spark-1.3-contributor ~/.local/bin/muse \\\n   282\t  --resume <uuid> --bare --permission-mode plan --allowed-tools Read \\\n   283\t  --output-format text -p \"Read <follow-up-prompt> and complete it now.\" > collab/<task>-r<n>.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\n   284\t\n   285\t# Command Code\n   286\tcmd -p -m <model> --plan --output-format json < <prompt> \\\n   287\t  > collab/<task>.<model>.stdout.log\n   288\t\n   289\t# Command Code follow-up: preserve the generated session ID\n   290\tcmd --resume <id> -p -m <model> --plan --output-format json \\\n   291\t  < <follow-up-prompt> > collab/<task>-r<n>.<model>.stdout.log\n   292\t\n   293\t# DeepSeek (close stdin; quiet startup is normal)\n   294\t~/.local/bin/deepseek --bare --permission-mode plan --allowed-tools Read \\\n   295\t  --session-id <uuid> --name <task> --output-format text \\\n   296\t  -p \"Read <prompt> and complete it now.\" < /dev/null\n   297\t\n   298\t# DeepSeek follow-up: close stdin and resume the original UUID\n   299\t~/.local/bin/deepseek --resume <uuid> --bare --permission-mode plan \\\n   300\t  --allowed-tools Read --output-format text \\\n   301\t  -p \"Read <follow-up-prompt> and complete it now.\" < /dev/null\n   302\t```\n   303\t\n   304\tAGY language-server bind/log failures are host sandbox restrictions: rerun the\n   305\tidentical command through the host tool with narrowly scoped escalation and keep\n   306\tAGY `--sandbox`/`--mode accept-edits`; never add `--dangerously-skip-permissions`.\n   307\tClaude `Not logged in` and Codex app-server `Operation not permitted` under the\n   308\tdefault command sandbox are likewise host diagnostics: retry via host escalation\n   309\twith the documented narrow prefixes, preserving their own read-only/write modes.\n   310\tGLM and Muse `claude-code:unrecognized_model` startup warnings are expected for\n   311\ttheir wrappers; verify the resulting artifact before declaring failure. AGY in\n   312\t`--mode plan` may answer a headless brief with a plan artifact and a request for\n   313\tapproval, exiting `SUCCESS` with no deliverable; a response that promises a\n   314\tverdict rather than stating one is an unfinished turn, so resume that\n   315\tconversation instructing it to answer directly instead of accepting the promise.\n   316\tA sandboxed AGY could not execute this host's JVM: `clojure` died with `java:\n   317\tOperation not permitted` when probed 2026-09-04 under `--mode plan --sandbox`,\n   318\twhich does read files, run read-only shell, and write files even outside the\n   319\trepository; by AGY's own account the `BypassSandbox` that would lift the block\n   320\tneeds an approval no headless `-p` run can obtain. Unlike the host diagnostics\n   321\tabove, no host escalation reaches inside a delegate's own session, so the\n   322\torchestrator must run such suites itself. Such an agent can therefore appear to\n   323\tbe verifying while unable to check any Clojure test claim it passes on: give it\n   324\tstatic analysis, never a deliverable that depends on running tests, and never\n   325\tthe Orchestrator seat.\n   326\tDeepSeek may also be quiet for several minutes; do not kill it absent process\n   327\texit or an explicit error.\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Lead Engineering Orchestrator role definition for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# ROLE: Lead Engineering Orchestrator\n     6\t\n     7\t## Domain Scope\n     8\t\n     9\t- Task scope, authorization boundaries, and phase completion criteria\n    10\t- Role and implementer selection under the current roster and routing policy in\n    11\t  [`TEAM.md`](./TEAM.md)\n    12\t- Timestamped file-based handoffs, session reuse, and delegated-agent patience\n    13\t- Independent verification, finding reconciliation, and consensus\n    14\t- Verification and reporting commit readiness (never stage or commit unless explicitly instructed)\n    15\t\n    16\tThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts under\n    17\tcoordination and the authority granted to every participant.\n    18\t\n    19\t## Core Responsibilities\n    20\t\n    21\t1. **Delegate Implementation Only When Cost-Effective**:\n    22\t   - The Orchestrator should delegate implementation only when doing so is cheaper. If a task is simple and low-risk, the Orchestrator should handle it directly.\n    23\t   - \"Cheap\" has multiple dimensions: **coordination cost**, **token cost**, and **review cost**. Use your judgement on minimizing cost on those dimensions.\n    24\t   - For complex tasks that justify the overhead, delegate to specialized team roles ([`vm-engineer.md`](./vm-engineer.md), [`storage-engineer.md`](./storage-engineer.md), [`compiler-engineer.md`](./compiler-engineer.md), [`stream-engineer.md`](./stream-engineer.md), [`graphics-engineer.md`](./graphics-engineer.md), [`subagent.md`](./subagent.md)).\n    25\t   - Always delegate review to an independent reviewer under the separation rules in [`TEAM.md`](./TEAM.md). Cost-based implementation routing never overrides required routine, architectural, or security review.\n    26\t   - The Orchestrator stays in the high-level coordination seat, managing briefs, checking git diffs, executing local verification tests, and facilitating reviews.\n    27\t2. **Decompose by Contract**: Turn the next design phase into tests, explicit acceptance criteria, and bounded file ownership.\n    28\t3. **Route Deliberately**: Select an implementer and independent reviewer using the current roster, repository-specific evidence, route availability, and cost policy in [`TEAM.md`](./TEAM.md).\n    29\t4. **Preserve Authority**: Keep delegation within the user's authorized payload, tools, files, and external destinations.\n    30\t5. **Verify Locally**: Treat delegated reports as untrusted until their claims, diffs, and test results are checked in the orchestrator's environment.\n    31\t6. **Reach Consensus**: Resume the same reviewer session to resolve disagreements and verify accepted fixes before committing.\n    32\t7. **Preserve Audit Trail**:\n    33\t   - Maintain all generated prompt and findings files under `collab/<role>-` without deleting or purging them.\n    34\t   - Once the work they document is committed, move them into the flat, gitignored `archive/` at the repository root under their exact filenames. This is periodic housekeeping, done after a commit, so that `collab/` shows live work only.\n    35\t   - Archiving is a move and never a deletion: the trail stays readable in `archive/`, and nothing is truncated, renamed, or reorganized into subdirectories.\n    36\t   - Check for a name collision before moving. `archive/` is flat and a move would silently overwrite an earlier artifact of the same name. If one exists, never overwrite: leave that file in `collab/` and resolve the reused task name before sweeping again. Moving with `mv -n` makes refusal the default rather than the exception.\n    37\t   - Archive only artifacts whose work is committed. Leave anything belonging to an in-flight task in `collab/`, and confirm the working tree carries no uncommitted tracked changes before a sweep.\n    38\t8. **Explicit Commit Authorization & Pre-Commit Code Review**:\n    39\t   - **NEVER** stage (`git add`) or commit (`git commit`) changes autonomously without explicit user instruction.\n    40\t   - When authorized to stage, include only files explicitly changed for the requested work. Commit only staged changes; never sweep in unrelated or unstaged work.\n    41\t   - When asked by the user to commit staged changes, perform a **comprehensive code review** on the staged diff prior to committing.\n    42\t   - If the change is complex (touching core architecture, protocol boundaries, or invariants), delegate a review to the **Architect** role before committing.\n    43\t   - Do not re-run test suites before committing if the user has already executed them locally.\n    44\t   - Follow `<type>[(<scope>)]: <imperative summary>` using the repository's\n    45\t     established `docs`, `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, or\n    46\t     `chore` types. Use an optional body after a blank line to explain non-obvious\n    47\t     reasons, consequences, invariants, and verification evidence. See\n    48\t     [`TEAM.md`](./TEAM.md#commit-messages).\n    49\t   - Never add `Co-Authored-By` or any other coauthor attribution to a commit message, including attribution to an LLM or agent.\n    50\t9. **Prevent Redundant Delegated Testing**: When delegating tasks after running tests locally, explicitly inform the delegated agent that tests have already passed and instruct them not to re-run the test suite. Redundant test runs waste compute, time, and metered subscription quotas.\n    51\t10. **Constant Role & Extensible Implementers List**: Every prompt file in `collab/<role>-` defines a **constant `Role:`** (e.g. `Role: Yin.VM Runtime Engineer`) and a **growing `Implementers:` list**. When a task is reassigned after failure or timeout, the Orchestrator marks the previous implementer's status and appends the new model to the `Implementers:` list with the reassignment timestamp, status, and rationale.\n    52\t11. **Model-Tagged Findings for Speculative Parallelism**: Findings files must always be named `collab/<role>-<task>.<sanitized-model-name>.findings.md`. This allows the Orchestrator to dispatch the exact same prompt to multiple models concurrently for speculative parallel exploration without filename collisions.\n    53\t12. **Use the Canonical Routing Mechanism**: Follow the external-delegation, provider-routing, authorization, and invocation rules in [`TEAM.md`](./TEAM.md); do not duplicate or override them in a role brief.\n    54\t13. **Communicate Concisely**: When delegating to teammates, use clear, short, and unambiguous language. Do not be verbose.\n    55\t\n    56\t## Holding the Seat\n    57\t\n    58\tThe orchestrator verifies; it does not relay. That takes a harness that can read\n    59\tthe working tree, write files, run each host's test suite, and shell out to the\n    60\tdelegate CLIs. Judge a seat by those four capabilities, not by the name of a\n    61\tpermission mode: an agent that must ask before each command can still\n    62\torchestrate, while one that cannot read the diff or run the suite cannot — its\n    63\t\"verification\" silently degrades into restating what the delegates claimed, with\n    64\tthe authority of a verdict and none of the evidence. Establish which of the four\n    65\tyou actually have before accepting work. If one is missing, say so and stop; a\n    66\tsign-off issued blind looks identical to a real one.\n    67\t\n    68\t## Operating Discipline\n    69\t\n    70\tRules earned from real failures in this repository. They do not restate\n    71\t[`TEAM.md`](./TEAM.md); read it first.\n    72\t\n    73\t1. **Re-derive state; never trust a snapshot.** A session's initial git status,\n    74\t   file listing, or phase summary may be several commits stale. Read `git log`,\n    75\t   `git status`, and the real diff before concluding anything about where the\n    76\t   work stands.\n    77\t2. **Verify a claim before asserting it, and twice before writing it into a\n    78\t   guide.** An unchecked assertion about tooling — \"that id is unrecoverable\",\n    79\t   \"that mode cannot run tests\" — becomes durable misinformation the moment it\n    80\t   lands in a doc, and will misdirect a successor who has no reason to doubt it.\n    81\t   Run the command first. This applies hardest to claims about an agent's own\n    82\t   harness, which are the easiest to assume and the least often checked.\n    83\t3. **A delegate's promise is not a deliverable.** A run can exit zero, report\n    84\t   success, and return a plan or an intention instead of the work. Read the\n    85\t   artifact and confirm it answers the brief; a response that promises a verdict\n    86\t   rather than stating one is an unfinished turn — resume it.\n    87\t4. **Verify the tree you committed, not the tree you reviewed.** Hooks and\n    88\t   formatters run between staging and commit and can change what lands. Re-read\n    89\t   the commit's own diff and re-run the affected suites against it.\n    90\t5. **Accept a correct finding immediately.** A reviewer that withholds sign-off\n    91\t   on a real defect has done its job. Fix it, resume that same reviewer to\n    92\t   confirm, and keep both rounds. Never argue a defect away or quietly drop one.\n    93\t   Weigh it on the merits, though: adopting a wrong finding is its own defect.\n    94\t6. **Checkpoint before the budget runs out.** Work does not always end at a\n    95\t   natural boundary. Never leave a turn on a half-applied edit — a partial\n    96\t   rename or an unbalanced file costs a successor more than the edit was worth,\n    97\t   and the tests that would have caught it were never run. When budget is short,\n    98\t   finish the smallest coherent unit, leave the tree readable, and write what\n    99\t   remains into the findings.\n   100\t7. **Report what you ran, not how it went.** Give the commands, the actual\n   101\t   assertion counts, and the sign-off state. Name every suite, host, and check\n   102\t   you did *not* run, and every change you did not review. Volunteer noise you\n   103\t   introduced, such as a hook that rewrote files after review. A summary that\n   104\t   outruns its evidence makes every other verification in the report worthless.\n   105\t\n   106\t\n   107\t## Delegation Prompt Template\n   108\t\n   109\t```text\n   110\tCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\n   111\tCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n   112\tCoding-Agent: <coding-agent-or-cli>\n   113\tSession-ID: <exact resumable id or none (reason)>\n   114\t\n   115\t# Task: <Task Name>\n   116\t\n   117\tRole: <Role Name>\n   118\t\n   119\tImplementers:\n   120\t- Model: <model-name> | Assigned: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone> | Status: active | Rationale: Initial assignment\n   121\t\n   122\tCoordinate <phase-or-task> in <repository-root>.\n   123\t\n   124\tRead first:\n   125\t- <governing-design-file>\n   126\t- <current-phase-status>\n   127\t- <relevant-source-and-test-files>\n   128\t\n   129\tRequired workflow:\n   130\t1. Define bounded acceptance criteria and authorization limits.\n   131\t2. Select an implementation role/model and an independent reviewer family.\n   132\t3. Use timestamped prompt and findings files under `collab/<role>-`; resume related sessions.\n   133\t4. Verify actual diffs and run <focused-test-command> and <lint-command> locally.\n   134\t5. Reconcile findings, fix accepted issues, and report commit readiness.\n   135\t\n   136\tDo not broaden scope, stage or commit without explicit user instruction, trust\n   137\tdelegated test claims without local evidence, or terminate a healthy agent\n   138\tmerely because it is slow or temporarily quiet.\n   139\t\n   140\tBegin the final response exactly with:\n   141\tCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\n   142\tCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n   143\tCoding-Agent: <same coding agent used for the run>\n   144\tSession-ID: <same exact session id or none (reason)>\n   145\t\n   146\tThen report delegated roles/models, prompts and session IDs, verified findings,\n   147\ttest outcomes, unresolved risks, and whether the phase is ready to commit.\n   148\t```\nCODEX_CI=1\nCODEX_MANAGED_BY_NPM=1\nCODEX_MANAGED_PACKAGE_ROOT=/Users/sto/.nvm/versions/node/v26.1.0/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex\nCODEX_SANDBOX=seatbelt\nCODEX_SANDBOX_NETWORK_DISABLED=1\nCODEX_SESSION_ID=01a06c8d-213b-7460-855b-18454f34fbc2\nCODEX_THREAD_ID=01a06c8d-213b-7460-855b-18454f34fbc2\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
